@page "/Projects/HollowKnightSlice"

<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/HollowKnightSlice" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
      <StickyNote Text="Demo" OpenTo="StickyNote.TargetType.BlankPage" Link="https://36831.hosts2.ma-cloud.nl"></StickyNote>
    <h3>Project description</h3>
    
    <span>
      We weren't present at the original reveal of all the possible slice projects but once I got a whiff of all the entries. I knew instantly which game I wanted to make a replica off: Hollow knight. Since most of the group was chosen for the international collaboration project. There wasn't much time left to work together at school.
    </span>
    </div>
    <h3>The first two weeks</h3>
    <span>
      Nonetheless I tried to get the team through. Together we ended up with a workflow that felt more between kaban and waterfall in. Because we were all aware we only had 2 weeks before the interruption of the collaboration project. We sat together the first day just planning, figuring out what was needed and writing user stories (that were way too big looking back now). But we had a game plan. From then on out we had standups every day and jumped in quickly to help one and other if we felt it was nessescary. We finished the first sprint with a decent draft and confidence we could make the deadline.
    </span>
    <h3>
      The two weeks after
    </h3>
    <span>
      Oh boy did we underestimated how rough coming back to this project was. After going through all the code and looking at the art we realized that there was a lot of work to be done. So we spent a day inventorizing everything and made a new planning for the upcoming two weeks. It was going to be rough. But we still made it. It doesn't win a beauty contest but we have a finished demo.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

